I thought whether you got infravision was entirely
dependent on what race you were.  If so, there's no reason
to start-scum for it; just pick the race you want in the
first place.  And, indeed, of the two races with the
"good" numbers, above, Human and Dwarf, one of them has
infravision and the other does not.

Infravision *could* be one of the reasons why a lot of
wizards are played as elves.  Orcs have undesirable stat
limitations for a wizard (mainly, low intelligence), so if
you're looking for advantages (as opposed to flavor or a
challenge) you wouldn't choose an orcish wizard character.
Of the three remaining options, humans and elves are the
only ones that can be chaotic, which is arguably the
preferred alignment for wizards.  Elves have a couple of
advantages over humans.  One is that they can get higher
intelligence (a particularly useful stat for wizards);
another is that they have infravision, which is useful
for any role, especially in the Mines.

Personally I don't prefer playing as an Elf, because they
have a hard time *carrying* much in the early game, which
is a problem for me; but maybe that's because I haven't
fully learned to do ideal inventory management yet.  A
more advanced player might have a better sense of item
weights and a better ability to predict which items will
be needed sooner rather than later.


